  "query_preview": "..\nPleasant weather of isolated showers and light to gentle\nnortheast to east winds look to continue in the short-term forecast.\nIsland-effect showers and isolated thunderstorms during the\nafternoons will be the main concern for Guam through the weekend. \nAlthough there is a background trade-wind pattern over the region, \nwind speeds may subside enough for a light to gentle wind pattern \nover Guam, therefore allowing these showers to grow directly overhead\ninstead of advecting offshore. As for next week, model guidance \nindicates a potential tropical disturbance bringing increasing shower\nand thunderstorm chances. The forecast reflects only scattered \nshowers of 30 percent and a slight chance of thunderstorms for now, \nbut this will likely be increased as model solutions converge on \ntiming and intensity over the next few days. Those with interests in \nthe Marianas should keep a close eye on the forecast over the next \nseveral days. Although we've had a lull in tropical cyclone activity,\nthis should be a reminder that the Marianas are in peak typhoon \nseason, so maintaining a background state of preparedness is crucial.",
